          "angle": 180
        },
        {
          "id": "current-synthesis",
          "label": "Current Synthesis",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "OpenSim as a 3D Host Candidate",
        "description": "One fragment of a design conversation observed that Second Life/OpenSim already has an object model shaped like a graph, and asked whether Twilite could reach a 3D host by translating to it instead of inventing a 3D world model from nothing. This topic checks that against what Twilite already has, and lists what would need to be researched before treating OpenSim as more than a candidate.",
        "scope": "OpenSim (opensim/opensim on GitHub) as a possible renderer/host behind Twilite's planned `3d` View host tag. Not in scope: VR/AR/map hosts, or building a 3D renderer of our own.",
        "overview": "Twilite already reserves a `3d` host tag on Views (`VIEW_HOSTS` in `surfaceChains.js`) with nothing behind it yet. OpenSim is an open-source, C#, self-hostable virtual-world simulator whose scene model already separates a Region/Scene into `SceneObjectGroup`s (linksets) made of `SceneObjectPart`s (prims), one of them the root, plus avatars as `ScenePresence`. That is structurally close to a graph, though it is not stored as one.",
        "keyQuestions": "Does `3d` need a shared native model, or only an adapter at the boundary? What of a Twilite graph does not translate (multi-parent edges, non-spatial relationships, behavior that is not a script)? What would it cost to stand up and would the license allow it? Is OpenSim the right comparison at all, or should a browser-native 3D scene graph (three.js, Babylon.js) be evaluated first?",
        "documentation": "This graph is also a worked example of the Topic template. Its Topic, Concept, Claim, Mechanism, Perspective, Source, and Synthesis nodes use the v0.3 additive documentation fields and semantic ports.",
        "definitionKey": "topic",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "topic",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic.node-class.node"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "opensim-3d-host-candidate"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "osim-c-scene",
      "type": "topic-concept",
      "label": "Concept",
      "position": {
        "x": -1000,
        "y": 220
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        },
        {
          "id": "mechanism",
          "label": "Mechanism",
          "direction": "output",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 345
        },
        {
          "id": "claims",
          "label": "Claims",
          "direction": "output",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 15
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "OpenSim's Scene Model",
        "definition": "`SceneGraph` (in OpenSim's source, a class once called `InnerScene`) holds the entities in a Region: `SceneObjectGroup`s indexed by UUID and local id, `ScenePresence`s (avatars), the physics scene, and running scripts. A `SceneObjectGroup` is one in-world object, made of one or more `SceneObjectPart`s (\"prims\"), with exactly one part designated the root; physics and linking operations act on the \"root of a linkset.\"",
        "documentation": "Sketch:\n\n`Region / Scene`\n  `SceneGraph`\n    `SceneObjectGroup` (root `SceneObjectPart` + parts) — a linkset\n    `SceneObjectGroup` …\n    `ScenePresence` (avatar) …\n\nThis is an object-oriented model built for Second Life semantics (groups, root parts, linksets, parent references, attachments, ownership, inventories, regions), not a generic `{ nodes: [], edges: [] }` structure — but mathematically it is graph-shaped.",
        "examples": "A chair with a cushion and two pillows linked together is one SceneObjectGroup (one linkset) with four SceneObjectParts; whichever part was linked first (or set as root) carries the group's position and receives physics and script authority for the set.",
        "notes": "Read from the design conversation's description of the OpenSim source, not independently read by me against the opensim/opensim repository. Flagged unverified in the synthesis.",
        "definitionKey": "topic-concept",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "topic-concept",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-concept.node-class.node"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "opensim-3d-host-candidate"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "osim-c-gap",
      "type": "topic-concept",
      "label": "Concept",
      "position": {
        "x": -480,
        "y": 220
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
