    {
      "id": "osim-claim-shaped",
      "type": "topic-claim",
      "label": "Claim",
      "position": {
        "x": -1000,
        "y": 620
      },
      "width": 460,
      "height": 340,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "Concept",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180
        },
        {
          "id": "perspective",
          "label": "Perspective",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "source",
          "label": "Source",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 90
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Graph-shaped is not graph-represented",
        "claim": "OpenSim's object model is mathematically graph-shaped (objects, parts, a designated root, containment) but is not stored, serialized, or exposed as a generic `{nodes, edges}` structure. Any correspondence with Twilite has to be built, not assumed.",
        "rationale": "The model is expressed through domain-specific C# types and operations (linksets, root parts, physics, ownership) rather than a generic graph API. Twilite gains nothing by pattern-matching the shape unless it also builds and maintains the translation.",
        "evidence": "The design conversation's own reading of the OpenSim source: a `SceneGraph` class, `SceneObjectGroup`/`SceneObjectPart` types, and physics/linking code that refers explicitly to \"the root of a linkset.\"",
        "caveats": "This claim rests on one secondhand reading of the source, not my own. It should be re-verified against the actual `opensim/opensim` repository before being treated as settled — see the open questions.",
        "documentation": "",
        "confidence": "low",
        "definitionKey": "topic-claim",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "topic-claim",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-claim.node-class.node"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "opensim-3d-host-candidate"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "osim-claim-adapter",
      "type": "topic-claim",
      "label": "Claim",
      "position": {
        "x": -460,
        "y": 620
      },
      "width": 460,
      "height": 340,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "Concept",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180
        },
        {
          "id": "perspective",
          "label": "Perspective",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "source",
          "label": "Source",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 90
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "The integration point is a translator, not a shared model",
        "claim": "If Twilite ever hosts a 3D View through OpenSim, the right shape is `Twilite graph → adapter → OpenSim scene`, an explicit, one-directional (or reconciled) translation layer — not making Twilite's own node model conform to OpenSim's, or vice versa.",
        "rationale": "Twilite's primitives (Declaration, Port, View, Content, Glyph, Portal, Bridge) serve graphs generally, most of which are never spatial. OpenSim's model serves one specific simulator. Coupling them directly would either drag simulator concepts into every Twilite graph or force every Twilite concept through a 3D lens.",
        "evidence": "",
        "caveats": "An adapter still has to resolve every case in gm-map that is not one-to-one, and has to decide what happens when a graph changes on one side without a corresponding change on the other.",
        "documentation": "",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "definitionKey": "topic-claim",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "topic-claim",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-claim.node-class.node"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "opensim-3d-host-candidate"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "osim-mech",
      "type": "topic-mechanism",
      "label": "Mechanism",
      "position": {
        "x": 40,
        "y": 620
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 380,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "How a Twilite-to-OpenSim adapter would work",
        "mechanism": "An adapter layer sits between a Twilite graph and a running OpenSim simulator, translating in the shape sketched in gm-map: Node ↔ SceneObject, Edge ↔ relationship/link/constraint, Property ↔ object property, Method ↔ script action, Event ↔ simulator event, and the graph's Detail surface ↔ the region/environment experience a visitor sees.",
        "conditions": "OpenSim exposes a way for an external process to create/update objects and receive events (its own scripting layer, and/or a network API) — not yet confirmed. Twilite's authority and mutation model (`declaration.authority`) would need to say who may write into a live simulator, which is a stronger claim than writing into a graph file.",
        "steps": "1. A Twilite graph declares a Surface with a `3d` host.\n2. The adapter walks the graph and creates/updates the corresponding OpenSim objects in a region.\n3. OpenSim events (touch, collision, chat) are translated back and raised as Twilite Events on the owning node.\n4. A visitor connects to the region with an OpenSim-compatible viewer, not with the Twilite web client.",
        "failureModes": "A Twilite relationship with no spatial meaning (e.g. a Bridge, a Portal, a Field) has no natural OpenSim counterpart. A graph edit made while disconnected has to reconcile against simulator state that may have drifted. Round-tripping an in-world edit back into the graph is a second, harder translation the sketch does not cover.",
        "documentation": "",
        "definitionKey": "topic-mechanism",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "topic-mechanism",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-mechanism.node-class.node"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "opensim-3d-host-candidate"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "osim-persp",
      "type": "topic-perspective",
      "label": "Perspective",
      "position": {
        "x": 620,
        "y": 620
      },
      "width": 460,
      "height": 340,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "A shelved candidate, not a plan",
        "perspective": "Treat OpenSim as one candidate 3D host among possible others, parked on a shelf marked \"3D host candidate\" rather than committed to. The alternative the conversation did not evaluate is a browser-native 3D scene graph (three.js, Babylon.js, or similar), which would need no separate server process and no simulator protocol.",
        "distinction": "OpenSim gives an existing, working virtual-world server with avatars, physics and a viewer ecosystem already built — at the cost of running and maintaining a simulator, and translating through a model built for Second Life rather than for Twilite. A browser-native scene graph gives tighter integration with the existing web client, at the cost of building 3D-specific concepts (physics, avatars, multi-user presence) that OpenSim already has.",
        "implications": "This should not be decided from one design conversation. It belongs after the resolver and Page Template work, evaluated against whatever the first real `3d` View actually needs to do.",
        "documentation": "",
        "definitionKey": "topic-perspective",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "topic-perspective",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-perspective.node-class.node"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "opensim-3d-host-candidate"
        }
      }
    },
