    {
      "id": "osim-synth",
      "type": "topic-synthesis",
      "label": "Synthesis",
      "position": {
        "x": 180,
        "y": 1080
      },
      "width": 620,
      "height": 440,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "A real structural echo; nothing here decides anything yet",
        "summary": "OpenSim's object model plausibly rhymes with a graph (Region → SceneObjectGroup/root part → parts, plus avatars), which is worth knowing. But every fact about OpenSim in this graph traces back to one unverified, secondhand paraphrase, and the proposed mapping is a sketch, not a contract. Twilite's own `3d` host slot is real and currently empty.",
        "implications": "Nothing here should be built yet. The next step is research, not implementation, and it does not block the resolver or Page Template work.",
        "openQuestions": "What was pasted is one fragment; earlier context (\"your antenna went up\") was not seen and may contain claims not repeated here.\n\n**To make this happen, research would need to answer:**\n1. Read the actual `opensim/opensim` repository and confirm (or correct) the `SceneGraph`/`SceneObjectGroup`/`SceneObjectPart` description firsthand.\n2. What OpenSim actually offers an external process for creating, updating and receiving events for objects: its own script layer, a REST/RPC surface, or only the avatar viewer protocol (LLUDP)?\n3. Can OpenSim be run headless, embedded, or otherwise made lightweight, or does a useful instance require a full grid/region deployment and its own hosting cost?\n4. OpenSim's license (checked, not assumed) and whether it is compatible with how Twilite is distributed.\n5. Is a browser-native 3D scene graph (three.js, Babylon.js) a better-fitting comparison, given Twilite's web client already exists and a simulator would not need to be run at all?\n6. Which Twilite relationships have no spatial meaning (Bridge, Portal, Field, non-spatial Edges) and what an adapter does with them.\n7. What Twilite's authority/mutation model would need to say about writing into a live, shared simulator, as opposed to a graph file.\n8. Whether round-tripping an in-world change back into the Twilite graph is even attempted, or whether the graph stays the one-way source of truth.",
        "documentation": "",
        "definitionKey": "topic-synthesis",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "topic-synthesis",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-synthesis.node-class.node"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "opensim-3d-host-candidate"
        }
      }
    }
